text:
This looks like Portugal: Portuguese-style granite cobbled sidewalk, white-plastered apartment blocks, the yellow-and-black striped bollards, and the glass tower in the distance resembling office buildings in Porto's Boavista/Foco area. Right-hand traffic, deciduous trees in winter and bright Atlantic light fit northern Portugal. The mix of mid-rise residential blocks and the distinctive blue-glass tower suggests the Boavista business district near Avenida da Boavista in Porto.

>>>
{"country":"Portugal","place":"Porto, Boavista area","lat":41.1600,"lng":-8.6400}

White Chevrolet cars everywhere (Malibu, Cobalt, Damas van) are the hallmark of Uzbekistan, where Chevrolet dominates the market. Wide boulevard with green medians, Soviet-era apartment blocks with ground-floor shops, and the "KÖR"-style Latin-script Uzbek billboards plus Uzbek phone codes (97, 95) confirm it. The scale and style of the intersection suggests central Tashkent.

>>>
{"country":"Uzbekistan","place":"Tashkent","lat":41.3111,"lng":69.2797}

This is unmistakably Edinburgh: Victorian sandstone tenements with bay windows, left-hand driving, UK traffic lights, a Co-op Funeralcare shop, and the hill in the distance resembles Arthur's Seat/Salisbury Crags. The street layout and view toward the crags suggest the Southside/Newington area, likely looking along Marchmont Road or Causewayside toward the Grange.

>>>
{"country":"United Kingdom","place":"Edinburgh, Marchmont/Newington","lat":55.9385,"lng":-3.1875}

This looks like a Balkan urban courtyard parking lot: mixed older Austro-Hungarian-style apartment blocks with red tile roofs, a modern glass tower, a VW T4 van, a VW Golf, and an Audi with a plate format consistent with Serbia. The somewhat worn walls, tin-roofed outbuildings and dense inner-city blocks suggest central Belgrade, likely around Vračar or Dorćol.

>>>
{"country":"Serbia","place":"Belgrade","lat":44.8060,"lng":20.4690}

This is the China Children's Store (中国儿童用品商店), a well-known landmark on Wangfujing pedestrian street in Beijing, with simplified Chinese signage and Spring Festival "蛇舞迎春" (Year of the Snake) decorations on the red pillars. The adjacent Longines boutique and 24h self-service bank fit the Wangfujing shopping corridor.

>>>
{"country":"China","place":"Wangfujing Street, Dongcheng, Beijing","lat":39.9134,"lng":116.4113}

This is a monumental white neoclassical/interwar building with pilasters, typical of 1930s Romanian institutional architecture, and the vehicles (Opel Astra, BMW, Mercedes) with Eastern European parking habits fit Bucharest. The building strongly resembles the Faculty of Law of the University of Bucharest on Bulevardul Mihail Kogălniceanu, with its colonnaded facade and side wings. Plane trees and a camper parked by a scruffy green verge match Bucharest's streets.

>>>
{"country":"Romania","place":"Bucharest, Faculty of Law, Bd. Mihail Kogălniceanu","lat":44.4356,"lng":26.0862}

The skyline with the Commerzbank Tower, Messeturm-like spire and other high-rises is unmistakably Frankfurt am Main, viewed from the Sachsenhausen side of the Main. German traffic lights, yield sign and blue mandatory-direction sign confirm Germany; the bridge approach with sandstone parapets and plane trees matches the southern end of the Untermainbrücke at Schaumainkai.

>>>
{"country":"Germany","place":"Frankfurt am Main, Untermainbrücke / Schaumainkai (Sachsenhausen)","lat":50.1067,"lng":8.6745}

This is an Italian railway station platform: Trenitalia-style departure board showing "ANCONA" as destination, Italian regional train livery, travertine-clad rationalist station architecture, and rows of scooters parked outside. The long platform with travertine walls and canopy matches Firenze Santa Maria Novella, from which regional trains run to Ancona via Faenza; the gray wet weather fits Tuscany in winter.

>>>
{"country":"Italy","place":"Firenze Santa Maria Novella station, Florence","lat":43.7764,"lng":11.2481}

Rough granite cobblestone street, Volvo V60 Cross Country, Lexus NX, Subaru — a Baltic/Nordic feel with red-brick apartment buildings reflected in the windows. The castellated stone façade with art-deco-like ornamentation and the setts strongly suggest Riga's quieter central streets near the Old Town or Jugendstil quarter, Latvia. Wet overcast weather fits the Baltic climate.

>>>
{"country":"Latvia","place":"Riga","lat":56.9540,"lng":24.1120}

French setting: Renault Clio with company livery "Connan" and phone number starting 05 62, which is the Hautes-Pyrénées/Gers area code; slate steep roofs and half-timbered gable with brick trim suggest Béarn/Bigorre foothills. Yellow painted curbs, French "sens interdit" sign and red-white striped corner marker fit a town in southwest France near the Pyrenees. Tarbes/Lourdes area fits the 05 62 prefix and architecture.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Lourdes area, Hautes-Pyrénées","lat":43.0950,"lng":-0.0450}

This looks like Myanmar: right-hand traffic with a mix of cars (many Japanese imports), concrete median blocks painted with black-and-white stripes, wide concrete road, dense tropical greenery, and modest multi-storey buildings typical of Yangon or Mandalay. The low concrete block median and tall wooden/concrete utility poles with heavy wire bundles are characteristic of Yangon's broad arterial roads, and the flat terrain with lush vegetation suits the Yangon area rather than drier Mandalay.

>>>
{"country":"Myanmar","place":"Yangon","lat":16.8409,"lng":96.1735}

Granite cobblestone street (pé-de-moleque style), stone retaining wall with lush tropical vegetation and tall trees, and a Mercedes Sprinter van are typical of hillside neighborhoods in Rio de Janeiro. The walled villa with wrought-iron fence, colonial-style gate, and steep leafy lane strongly suggests Santa Teresa or Cosme Velho/Laranjeiras. I'll commit to Santa Teresa.

>>>
{"country":"Brazil","place":"Santa Teresa, Rio de Janeiro","lat":-22.9195,"lng":-43.1880}

This is Khao San Road area in Bangkok: the "The One Khaosan" bar with its distinctive A-frame lattice roof, the Biggain Tattoo shop, souvenir stalls, tourists in shorts, and Thai-style shophouses with awnings. The street-market vibe and backpacker crowd confirm Banglamphu district.

>>>
{"country":"Thailand","place":"Khao San Road, Bangkok","lat":13.7589,"lng":100.4973}

This looks like a Baltic city: Soviet-era panel apartment blocks, a Lidl store, blue lane-direction signs above traffic lights, priority road diamond signs, and the very wide intersection design typical of Lithuania. The ladder-style crosswalk markings with grey poles and the cycle path with yellow tactile paving strongly resemble Vilnius or Kaunas residential districts; the layout and block style suggest a Vilnius sleeping district such as Pašilaičiai/Fabijoniškės.

>>>
{"country":"Lithuania","place":"Vilnius, Fabijoniškės/Pašilaičiai","lat":54.7260,"lng":25.2410}

Hungarian text on the red billboard ("Csökkentse vállalkozása áramdíját!" – an E.ON ad) pins this to Hungary, with right-hand traffic and a wide multi-lane urban arterial with a blue lane-direction sign typical of Budapest. The ochre two-storey building with chimneys and the mansard-roofed block ahead, plus hills in the haze, suggest the Buda side near a major radial road such as Budaörsi út / Hegyalja út area or the Szentendrei út corridor in Óbuda.

>>>
{"country":"Hungary","place":"Budapest (Óbuda, Szentendrei út area)","lat":47.5510,"lng":19.0450}

This looks like a Nordic institutional campus: a large curved beige building with an industrial-style roof beside a modern dark-brick block with glass atrium, gravel parking, birch trees, autumn light and European plates on Volvo/Toyota cars. The architecture resembles a Finnish hospital or university campus — specifically the curved building recalls the Kuopio University Hospital / Savonia area, with the new dark extension typical of recent Finnish hospital builds.

>>>
{"country":"Finland","place":"Kuopio, Puijonlaakso hospital area","lat":62.8946,"lng":27.6369}

Variable speed limit signs showing "70" on an overhead gantry, concrete overpass, and a wide motorway with Belgian-style lighting strongly suggest the Brussels or Antwerp ring road in Belgium. The yellow truck with blue "ux" lettering looks like a Luxembourg or Benelux hauler, and the flat green terrain with dense traffic matches the Antwerp Ring (R1), where 70 km/h variable limits are common.

>>>
{"country":"Belgium","place":"Antwerp Ring (R1), Antwerp","lat":51.2070,"lng":4.4270}

This looks like a Baltic/Nordic city courtyard: limestone rubble walls with old brick patches and graffiti, a gravel lot, chestnut trees, and a modern angular timber-clad structure typical of Tallinn's creative quarters. The limestone construction is characteristic of Tallinn, Estonia, where such walls appear widely in Kalamaja/Kesklinn backyards near newer glass-fronted office buildings.

>>>
{"country":"Estonia","place":"Tallinn, Kesklinn","lat":59.4330,"lng":24.7420}

Yellow dashed centre line and tram/trolleybus overhead wires are classic Swiss urban markings, and the historic neo-Renaissance stone buildings with scaffolding plus modern corten-steel university buildings point to Zurich's university district. The street looks like Rämistrasse/Gloriastrasse area near ETH/University of Zurich, with the Swiss-style bus/tram shelter and dense bike parking typical of a campus.

>>>
{"country":"Switzerland","place":"Zurich, Hochschulquartier (Rämistrasse/Gloriastrasse)","lat":47.3764,"lng":8.5485}

Georgian script on the car wash sign ("თვითრეცხვა" = self-service wash, "ბაბლ" = Bubble) firmly places this in Georgia. The unfinished concrete-block construction, flat urban terrain, sunny dry climate, and a Fiat Ducato camper van suggest a Tbilisi suburb, possibly along a main road where car washes cluster.

>>>
{"country":"Georgia","place":"Tbilisi","lat":41.7151,"lng":44.8271}

Greek signage ("ΕΜΠΟΡΙΑ", "Σταύρος Στ. Μαργαρίτης") confirms Greece, and the dense mid-century polykatoikia apartment blocks with heavy graffiti, narrow commercial lanes with shoe shops, and a Euronet-style ATM point strongly to central Athens. The style of street—narrow, commercial, with small shops and pedestrian activity—matches the Psiri/Monastiraki area near Athinas and Ermou streets.

>>>
{"country":"Greece","place":"Athens, Psiri/Monastiraki area","lat":37.9785,"lng":23.7265}

Traditional Chinese signage, green street-name signs with Taiwan-style bilingual labels ("熱河一街 Rehe 1st St" and "青島街 Qingdao St"), scooters everywhere, and the typical narrow concrete shophouses point to Taiwan. The 美而美 breakfast chain and a "彰化肉圓" shop are quintessentially Taiwanese. Rehe 1st Street and Qingdao Street intersect in Sanmin District, Kaohsiung, near the main station; the 美而美 sign even says "熱河店".

>>>
{"country":"Taiwan","place":"Sanmin District, Kaohsiung (Rehe 1st St / Qingdao St)","lat":22.6377,"lng":120.3118}

This is Vienna: the green "CAT City Airport Train" City Check-In sign and "Wien Mitte" lettering on the brown building identify the Wien Mitte station area at Landstraßer Hauptstraße / Marxergasse. The glass office towers (Bahnhof Wien Mitte complex, "The Mall"), Viennese street sign plate reading "3., Marxergasse"-style, and typical Austrian pedestrian signals confirm it.

>>>
{"country":"Austria","place":"Vienna, Wien Mitte (Landstraße), Marxergasse","lat":48.2066,"lng":16.3853}

This is a classic Copenhagen streetscape: granite-curbed sidewalks with concrete slab and cobble strips, massive numbers of parked bicycles with child seats, rainbow flags (Copenhagen Pride), and overhead catenary street lamps hung on wires. The large beige modernist office blocks and dark brick building suggest the area around Vesterbrogade/Kalvebod Brygge near the central station, possibly the SAS/Vester Farimagsgade district.

>>>
{"country":"Denmark","place":"Copenhagen, Vester Farimagsgade area","lat":55.6765,"lng":12.5655}

This is a Scandinavian waterfront with granite cobblestone quay, chain bollards, moored older ships, and a large corten steel arch sculpture. The red/pink building edge and the setting resemble the Göteborgsoperan (Gothenburg Opera) area at Lilla Bommen / Packhuskajen in Gothenburg, Sweden, with the rocky Hisingen shoreline and ships across the Göta älv in the background.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Gothenburg, Lilla Bommen / Opera quay","lat":57.7110,"lng":11.9640}

The billboard text "Ianao no boss" and "1 taona" is Malagasy, and the small "BNI Madagascar" plus Orange "1Go" ad confirm Madagascar; right-hand driving, a Renault 4 and swarms of scooters fit Antananarivo. The wide divided boulevard with billboards and a Ford dealership sign suggests the Route des Hydrocarbures/Ankorondrano commercial zone in the capital.

>>>
{"country":"Madagascar","place":"Antananarivo (Ankorondrano)","lat":-18.8830,"lng":47.5230}

The granite drinking fountain bears a coat of arms with a stylised cityscape under a shield — the emblem of Sarajevo — and the hillside houses with red roofs, dense forested slopes and socialist-era apartment blocks match Sarajevo's valley suburbs. The motorhome and camper-service station suggest a camping/service area on the city edge.

>>>
{"country":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","place":"Sarajevo","lat":43.8486,"lng":18.3564}

Yellow dashed centre line on a dedicated bike path with a parallel pedestrian lane, railway tracks with Norwegian-style catenary masts, and modern glass office blocks point strongly to Norway. The setting beside a rail yard with container depots and an elevated road/rail structure, plus new office development, matches the Lillestrøm/Drammen style redevelopments near stations; the blue-glass building and freight yard look like the Drammen station area along the Drammenselva bike route.

>>>
{"country":"Norway","place":"Drammen, Viken","lat":59.7405,"lng":10.2060}

Yellow Dutch licence plate, brick-paved street with red bike-lane edge, "zone 30" sign, cyclist, and Dutch-style brick house with steep tiled roof all point to the Netherlands. The gnome/grey coat cyclist and flat parkland with mature trees suggest a mid-sized city; the villa-like building and park layout resemble a Zuid-Holland or Utrecht town. I'll guess the Utrecht/Zeist area.

>>>
{"country":"Netherlands","place":"Zeist, Utrecht","lat":52.0906,"lng":5.2333}

Left-hand traffic, red Toyota Crown taxis, and dense clusters of tall residential towers with green-tinted glass are classic Hong Kong. The highway with white arrow chevrons and the Cheung Sha Wan/Lai Chi Kok style high-rises (e.g. Aqua Marine / Banyan Garden) plus construction on the left suggest West Kowloon Highway near Sham Shui Po / Nam Cheong.

>>>
{"country":"Hong Kong","place":"West Kowloon Highway near Cheung Sha Wan, Kowloon","lat":22.3290,"lng":114.1520}

Korean palace architecture: dark grey roof tiles with white-painted end caps, red-ochre painted wooden beams, and grey brick walls with the distinctive Joseon-era style. The green sign with Chinese characters starting with 景福 (Gyeongbok) strongly points to Gyeongbokgung Palace in Seoul, and the hexagonal grey brick structure resembles the base of Dongsipjagak watchtower or similar palace corner structures.

>>>
{"country":"South Korea","place":"Gyeongbokgung Palace, Seoul","lat":37.5759,"lng":126.9769}

Yellow-black striped curbs, manicured palm-lined medians, and the beige electrical substation box are hallmarks of Oman, specifically the well-landscaped expressways of Muscat. Right-hand driving, a hazy Gulf sky, and the low white building style fit the Muscat area along Sultan Qaboos Street with its flyover junctions.

>>>
{"country":"Oman","place":"Muscat (Al Khuwair / Sultan Qaboos Street area)","lat":23.5959,"lng":58.4059}

The warm honey-coloured limestone ashlar wall is classic Maltese globigerina limestone, and the perforated white metal public toilet facade (with "Public Convenience" sign) matches modern street furniture installed in Valletta/Floriana. Night lighting, Mediterranean creeper vegetation and the neat masonry fit Malta's capital area precisely.

>>>
{"country":"Malta","place":"Valletta","lat":35.8978,"lng":14.5125}

This looks like Luxembourg City: the large 1960s-70s modernist office block with grid facade on the left resembles buildings along Boulevard Royal / Avenue Marie-Thérèse near the Parc de la Ville, and the bare winter trees of a park on the right fit the city park alongside Boulevard Prince Henri. Right-hand driving, dashed white lane markings, low bollards and the overcast Benelux winter atmosphere all match, and the building with "LAWAI"-type signage and circular glass tower in the background match the Boulevard Royal banking district.

>>>
{"country":"Luxembourg","place":"Luxembourg City, Boulevard Prince Henri / Boulevard Royal","lat":49.6135,"lng":6.1250}

Left-hand traffic with a boxy truck bearing yellow/red chevron markings, concrete Jersey barriers, tall streetlights, and lush subtropical vegetation with Norfolk pines point to southern Africa. The setting resembles Maputo, Mozambique, specifically the Avenida de Moçambique / N1 corridor near the airport, with its concrete barrier medians and roadside minibus taxi activity.

>>>
{"country":"Mozambique","place":"Maputo, Avenida de Moçambique (N1)","lat":-25.9280,"lng":32.5680}

The overhead sign reads "Av. La Cultura", "Urb. La Campiña", "Calle Bolívar" — Avenida de la Cultura is the main artery of Cusco, Peru. Spanish signage ("Muebles"), unfinished rebar-topped brick buildings, dry highland light and bare Andean sky confirm the Peruvian sierra, and the wide divided avenue with countdown traffic lights matches Cusco's eastern districts (San Sebastián/Wanchaq).

>>>
{"country":"Peru","place":"Cusco, Avenida de la Cultura (San Sebastián)","lat":-13.5295,"lng":-71.9440}

"8 tímar" on the parking sign is Faroese (Icelandic would be "klst."), and the treeless green hills, overcast maritime weather, and industrial buildings point to the Faroe Islands. A Scania dealership and large commercial lots suggest the Tórshavn area, likely the industrial zone near the harbour/Hoyvík.

>>>
{"country":"Faroe Islands","place":"Tórshavn","lat":62.0150,"lng":-6.7850}

Red-brick townhouses with white shutters and stone trim, a long straight street with Art Nouveau-style facades and French-style parking bays point to northern France. The architecture resembles Lille/Amiens/Arras bourgeois streets; the white-shuttered brick and stone mix strongly suggests Amiens.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Amiens, Hauts-de-France","lat":49.8905,"lng":2.2965}

This is clearly Shanghai: the Art Deco granite building with Chinese characters "上...物金属" (Shanghai ... metals), the elevated Yan'an Road viaduct in the background, Chinese blue signage, and the typical Shanghai mix of 1930s stone architecture and 1990s towers. The grand bronze doors with number 342 match the former Continental Bank/ Guangdong Bank style on Sichuan Road near Yan'an East Road.

>>>
{"country":"China","place":"Sichuan Middle Road near Yan'an East Road, Huangpu District, Shanghai","lat":31.2335,"lng":121.4845}

Dense mid-rise apartment blocks with balconies, ornate black twin-lantern streetlights with painted ID numbers, suspended center-hung street lamp on a wire, and a Citroën C3 with Mercosur-style plate all point to Argentina, specifically Buenos Aires. The narrow one-way street lined with tipa/plane trees and the Spanish wall poster fit the Recoleta/Barrio Norte or Palermo area.

>>>
{"country":"Argentina","place":"Buenos Aires, Recoleta","lat":-34.5920,"lng":-58.3980}

This looks like Barcelona's Ronda Litoral near Port Vell: the red cable car cabin of the Transbordador Aeri del Port is visible top right, Washingtonia palms line the road, and the distinctive arched lamp structures of Passeig de Colom/Moll de la Fusta appear ahead. The large neoclassical building on the left matches the Duana/Port Authority area, with right-hand traffic and Spanish-style white Mini confirming Spain.

>>>
{"country":"Spain","place":"Barcelona, Ronda Litoral near Port Vell","lat":41.3758,"lng":2.1816}

This is a US freeway with concrete pavement, orange construction barrels, yellow left-edge line, and a dense skyline of glassy residential high-rises with tropical palms and lush roadside vegetation. The skyline matches downtown Miami/Brickell seen approaching from the west along I-395/SR 836, with the Signature Bridge construction cranes visible.

>>>
{"country":"United States","place":"Miami, Florida (I-395 / SR 836 approaching downtown)","lat":25.7845,"lng":-80.2050}

Sign reads "PLATS NR 31" — Swedish for "place/stall number 31" — marking this as a Swedish outdoor market square with flower stalls. Cobblestone setting, Hemköp-style green "H" sign in background, and the scale of the market strongly suggest Hötorget in central Stockholm, a well-known flower and produce market.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Hötorget, Stockholm","lat":59.3353,"lng":18.0632}

Black London taxis (LEVC TX), red double-decker buses, left-hand driving, UK traffic lights and railings clearly place this in central London. The brutalist/Portland stone office blocks and the wide junction match Victoria Street near Victoria station, looking east toward Westminster with the former Westminster City Hall area and Victoria Palace Theatre vicinity.

>>>
{"country":"United Kingdom","place":"Victoria Street, Westminster, London","lat":51.4975,"lng":-0.1385}

This looks like the Castle District of Veszprém, Hungary: the ochre and red Baroque-era buildings with high steep tiled roofs, gravel pedestrian square, and the distinctive red wall with arched barred windows match Vár utca near the Bishop's Palace and the cathedral area. The tourist crowd, wrought-iron lamps and Central European roof forms support a Hungarian historic citadel setting.

>>>
{"country":"Hungary","place":"Veszprém, Castle District (Vár utca)","lat":47.0978,"lng":17.9050}

Traditional Chinese signage (愛玉園, 玉石, 陶瓷藝品 — jade and ceramic crafts), right-hand driving, Taiwanese-style tiled shophouses with metal roller doors, and a wide boulevard lined with palms point to Taiwan. The jade shop plus steep cloud-capped mountains looming at the end of the road strongly suggest Hualien, famous for jade and backed by the Central Mountain Range. The broad palm-lined road with a central median fits Hualien City's main avenues like Zhongshan or Zhongzheng Road.

>>>
{"country":"Taiwan","place":"Hualien City","lat":23.9872,"lng":121.6015}

The building sign reads "Hegdehaugsveien 31" with tenants like "Klinikk Oslo" and "Kaffebrenneriet", plus tram tracks and overhead catenary, grey Nordic winter light, and typical Oslo apartment architecture. This is the Hegdehaugsveien/Bogstadveien area near Parkveien in Oslo, looking northwest toward Majorstuen.

>>>
{"country":"Norway","place":"Hegdehaugsveien, Oslo","lat":59.9220,"lng":10.7295}

This looks like Turkey: a Turkish flag on the apartment block, Red Crescent emblem on the box truck, and typical white Turkish plates. The palms, cypress, flat-roofed white mid-rise apartments with external AC units, and dusty vacant lot suggest a Mediterranean coastal city — Antalya's dense residential districts fit well.

>>>
{"country":"Turkey","place":"Antalya (Muratpaşa district)","lat":36.8870,"lng":30.7050}

Impark is a Canadian parking operator (Imperial Parking), and the hangTag app is Vancouver-based; the lot sign reads "Plaza of Nations Large," Lot 11188. Plaza of Nations is on False Creek near BC Place in Vancouver's downtown, with rates in Canadian dollars and event-rate notes consistent with stadium proximity.

>>>
{"country":"Canada","place":"Plaza of Nations, False Creek, Vancouver, British Columbia","lat":49.2745,"lng":-123.1055}

"OSTKURVE" graffiti is the Young Boys Bern ultras' stand name, and the scene shows a modern tram/bus terminal with concrete flat-roofed canopy beside railway tracks, typical of Bern's new quarters. The large 1970s housing blocks and new office buildings match Bern Brünnen/Westside area near the S-Bahn line, with a tram terminus and bus loop. Grey autumn weather and Swiss catenary masts fit.

>>>
{"country":"Switzerland","place":"Bern Brünnen Westside","lat":46.9440,"lng":7.3850}

Low brick houses with flat roofs, a Fiat 500 and old Fiat 128/Renault 12 wagon, Renault Kangoo, and a wide divided access road with guardrail point to Argentina. The dusty arid setting, tall plane-like trees, and single-storey brick construction with irrigation-fed greenery resemble Mendoza's urban periphery along Acceso Este/Sur. The red "Gomería"-style workshop and overhead wire tangle fit Greater Mendoza suburbs like Guaymallén or Godoy Cruz.

>>>
{"country":"Argentina","place":"Guaymallén, Mendoza","lat":-32.8900,"lng":-68.8000}

This is a Fiat Ducato-based McLouis Lagan motorhome parked on a gravel lot with Mediterranean vegetation: Aleppo pines, cypresses and dry scrub under a bright sky. The red tipper truck with Cyrillic-looking lettering, the Mercedes A-Class and the overall feel of the lot point to a Balkan Adriatic setting, most likely the Dalmatian coast of Croatia where such used-vehicle lots near Split/Zadar are common.

>>>
{"country":"Croatia","place":"Split area, Dalmatia","lat":43.5300,"lng":16.4700}

This is a US suburban office park scene with "Gateway Plaza" signage, Lime scooters, a dark-green decorative lamppost, and mountainous-west vegetation (lush irrigated lawn, deciduous trees under an overcast sky). The architecture and layout strongly resemble Boise, Idaho's downtown-adjacent office areas, where Lime scooters operate; "Gateway Plaza" style office complexes near Park Center/Front Street fit well.

>>>
{"country":"United States","place":"Boise, Idaho","lat":43.6150,"lng":-116.1990}

"Mađarska Retfala" is a neighborhood of Osijek, Croatia (Retfala district); KiK Textil-Diskont and Offertissima are present in Croatia. The parking lot and building resemble the Retfala shopping area near the Drava in western Osijek. Flat Slavonian landscape and Croatian-style signage support this.

>>>
{"country":"Croatia","place":"Osijek, Retfala","lat":45.5620,"lng":18.6560}

This is a park with autumn maple foliage, a grass-paver surface, and a red-brick medieval wall on a mound to the left, typical of Baltic old castle grounds. The sloping grassy ramparts and brick ruins resemble Cēsis or Kuldīga in Latvia, or Trakai/Kaunas in Lithuania; the mound and brick wall strongly suggest Cēsis Castle park area in Latvia.

>>>
{"country":"Latvia","place":"Cēsis","lat":57.3128,"lng":25.2706}

A gravel forest path through dense deciduous woodland (oaks, hornbeam, ivy) on a hillside with a cut earthen bank and timber edging suggests a managed park or hill path in temperate Central Europe. The vegetation and clay soil look like the Pannonian/sub-Alpine hill zone, typical of Zagreb's Medvednica slopes or similar Slovenian/Croatian hill parks. With no signage, I lean toward a Zagreb city hill park path such as Tuškanac/Cmrok.

>>>
{"country":"Croatia","place":"Zagreb, Tuškanac/Medvednica foothills","lat":45.8260,"lng":15.9690}

This looks like Czech Republic: Central European villas with steep roofs on a wooded hillside, a tall industrial chimney, and a VW van with a "Pohřební" (funeral service) inscription. The elevated road with concrete barriers, blue-capped railing, and hilly terrain resembles Ústí nad Labem or Karlovy Vary; the chimney and setting strongly suggest the Ústí area with its Mariánský most / Předmostí interchange.

>>>
{"country":"Czech Republic","place":"Ústí nad Labem","lat":50.6612,"lng":14.0431}

A narrow single-lane rural lane with a grass strip down the middle, lush pasture, hedgerows and mature oaks points to temperate northwest Europe. Wooden post-and-wire fencing, a wooden utility pole, and the slightly hilly green landscape with a tiled roof and palm-like shrub suggest Normandy or Brittany in northern France rather than the UK (no stone walls, pole style continental). The bocage-type field pattern fits Lower Normandy well.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Normandy, near Vire (Calvados)","lat":48.85,"lng":-0.89}

The steep Pyrenean valley with dense apartment blocks packed into the valley floor, slate-faced stone walls, and terraced slopes with scattered bordas strongly suggest Andorra. The view down over a town cluster with mountain backdrop resembles Encamp or the Escaldes-Engordany/Andorra la Vella area seen from a hillside residential road. The Dacia Dokker van and oak/ash trees fit the Mediterranean-alpine mix typical of Andorra at ~1200 m.

>>>
{"country":"Andorra","place":"Encamp area (hillside above the valley), Andorra","lat":42.5330,"lng":1.5820}

Arid Caribbean landscape with sandy soil, wind-bent palms, cinderblock walls, and Dutch Caribbean-style houses with colorful corrugated roofs strongly suggest the ABC islands. The flat terrain, dusty roadside, Chevrolet pickup, and bright pastel paint fit Aruba best, likely along a main road in the Noord or Santa Cruz area.

